'''amoretto''' (am-ọ̄-ret′tō), ''n.''; pl. ''amoretti'' (-ti). [It.: see ''amoret.''] '''1.''' A person enamored; a lover.
{{smaller block|The ''amoretto'' was wont to take his stand at one place where sat his mistress.
{{right|''Gayton'', Notes on Don Quixote, p. 47.}}}}
'''2.''' A little love; a cupid.
{{smaller block|A painting in which ''amoretti'' are plentiful.
{{right|''J. A. Symonds'', Greek Poets, p. 335. (''N. E. D.'')}}}}

'''amorevolous†''' (am-ọ̄-rev′ọ̄-lus), ''a.'' [< It. ''amorevole'', loving, < ''amore'', love: see ''amor.''] Loving; kind; charitable. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|He would leave it to the Princesse to show her cordial and ''amorevolous'' affection.
{{right|''Bp. Hacket'', Life of Abp. Williams, p. 161.}}}}

'''amorino''' (am-ọ̄-rē′nọ̄), ''n.''; pl. ''amorini'' (-ni). [It., dim. of ''amore'', love, cupid: see ''amor.''] A little love; a cupid. {{smaller|Applied to figures common in Roman decorative art, and in Renaissance and modern styles which are imitative of Roman art; also to merely decorative representations of children in works of art.}}

'''amorist''' (am′ọ̄-rist), ''n.'' [< ''amor'' + ''-ist.''] A lover; a gallant; an inamorato. Also written ''amourist.''
{{smaller block|<poem>Justle that skipping feeble ''amorist''
Out of your loves seat.</poem>
{{right|''Marston'', Antonio and Mellida, I., ii. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|Our gay ''amourists'' then could not always compose if they could write their billets-doux.
{{right|''I. D’Israeli'', Amen. of Lit., II. 168.}}}}

'''a-mornings†''' (ạ-môr′ningz), ''prep. phr.'' as ''adv.'' [< ''a''<sup>3</sup> + ''mornings'', adverbial gen. of ''morning.'' Cf. ''adays.''] In the morning; every morning.
{{smaller block|<poem>Such pleasant walks into the woods</poem>}}

